     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br></div><br>On Thursday, April 7, 2016 at 5:05:24 AM UTC+2, Jeff Stratford wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi everyone,</div><div><br></div><div>I am examining the prevalence of weevil infested acorns at the tree level over a latitudinal gradient. </div><div><br></div><div>I used this code to do a logistic regression (feel free to comment is this looks wrong):</div><div><br></div><div>rubraprev &lt;- glm(weev.prev~ lat, data=rubra, weights=no.acorns, family=&quot;binomial&quot;)</div><div>summary(rubraprev)</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>library(ggplot2)</div><div>ggplot(rubra, aes(lat, weev.prev)) +</div><div>    geom_point(shape=1) + ?)</div><div><br></div><div>And I&#39;ve reach the limits of what I could figure out. I know I used the fitted function... just not sure how to carry them over to the plot.</div><div><br></div><div>Data are below.</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks,</div><div><br></div><div>Jeff   </div><div><br></div><div><div>structure(list(species = structure(c(1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, </div><div>1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L), .Label = c(&quot;RO&quot;, &quot;WO&quot;), class = &quot;factor&quot;), </div><div>    no.acorns = c(42L, 50L, 50L, 50L, 49L, 50L, 51L, 50L, 50L, </div><div>    56L, 50L, 50L, 25L, 101L, 50L, 50L, 58L, 53L, 41L, 58L, 12L, </div><div>    32L, 50L, 50L, 60L, 56L, 50L, 25L, 51L, 51L, 35L, 55L, 55L, </div><div>    25L, 100